id: pm-x9r2h3
title: "pm validate reports nothing for a stored custom field definition the runtime registry drops, such as cli_flag '--' or a blank alias"
description: "Raised by the Codex review of PR #1772 (2026-11-23, third pass). The runtime field registry (normalizeRuntimeFieldDefinition in src/core/schema/runtime-schema.ts) returns null for a definition whose cli_flag normalizes to an empty token, an invalid value_schema or an empty key, and drops a blank alias. pm validate --check-metadata scans the resolved registry (scanFieldOptionCollisions in src/sdk/schema/field-option-collisions.ts), so such a stored definition is invisible: the field silently stops working and nothing tells the operator. PR #1772 closes the write paths (add-field, profile apply, rename-field and mutateWorkspaceSettings refuse an empty spelling with schema_field_flag_invalid), but definitions already stored inline or in schema/fields.json stay unreported. Duplicate check: searched validate dropped field definition empty cli_flag registry; pm-8patuh (closed) owns spelling refusals at write time."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 3
tags[3]: "area:schema",diagnostics,validation
created_at: "2026-11-23T11:55:14.460Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-27T04:40:02.558Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-27T04:03:36.041Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-27T04:03:36.041Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 60
acceptance_criteria: "validate --check-metadata reports every stored field definition the registry drops, with the key, the source (inline settings or schema/fields.json) and the reason; A blank or empty-normalizing alias is reported even though the definition itself resolves; A spec seeds each dropped shape and asserts the validate rows, with a negative control on the current release"
parent: pm-8jdc
risk: low
confidence: high
resolution: "The registry reads inspectRuntimeFieldDefinition, which returns the resolved definition and what it discarded; scanFieldDefinitionLosses reads the stored entries inline and in the fields file and reports validate_schema_field_definition_dropped and _partial rows with source, index, key and reason; schema file entries that are not objects are skipped with runtime_schema_<section>_entry_invalid instead of failing the settings read; a metadata key that names nothing falls back to the field key"
expected_result: A stored definition that the registry drops or truncates is named by validate with a repair hint
actual_result: "validate lists every dropped and partial shape with its source and position, both inline and in schema/fields.json; a null schema file entry keeps inline settings; published 2026.11.26 reports none of them and resets settings on a null entry"
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-8patuh,discovered_from,"2026-11-23T11:55:14.460Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-27T03:31:49.947Z","harness:claude-code","Fix (2026-11-27, branch fix/field-admission-diagnostics). The registry's per-definition normalizer is now inspectRuntimeFieldDefinition in src/core/schema/runtime-schema.ts: it returns the resolved definition plus a loss record (dropped: not_an_object | key_empty | cli_flag_empty | value_schema_invalid; ignored: one line per member it skips). normalizeRuntimeFieldDefinition reads it, so the diagnostic cannot disagree with the registry. pm validate --check-metadata calls scanFieldDefinitionLosses (src/sdk/schema/field-definition-losses.ts), which reads the stored entries themselves, inline in settings.json and in the configured fields file, and reports rows {source, index, key, dropped|ignored} with validate_schema_field_definition_dropped:<n> and validate_schema_field_definition_partial:<n>. Ignored members covered: blank or non-string aliases, aliases that are not a list, an unknown or non-string type (read as string), unknown commands, blank required_types, a non-string description, and an explicit metadata_key or front_matter_key that names nothing. Two defects found while probing the dropped shapes, fixed here: a null entry in schema/fields.json, statuses.json or types.json threw inside the normalizers, so readSettings failed its merge and every setting fell back to its default (settings_read_merge_failed); entries that are not objects are now skipped with runtime_schema_<section>_entry_invalid:<n>, which health reports. And a description that is not a string threw the same way. Second: a metadata_key that normalizes to nothing stored every value under the empty item key (the item file held a line with an empty key); it now falls back to the field key and validate reports it. Negative control on the published 2026.11.26 build: validate --check-metadata reports nothing for the same fields file and the null entry yields settings_read_merge_failed. Spec: tests/unit/sdk/schema/field-definition-losses.spec.ts (21 cases, including registry parity for every shape)."
  "2026-11-27T04:28:32.402Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 (gpt-6.2-sol xhigh, 2026-11-27) on e02d401c0: 7 findings on this item, all verified and fixed. (1) A null metadata_key no longer hides front_matter_key: the explicit key keeps the nullish precedence the old normalizer used. (2) An unreadable fields file (a directory) no longer crashes validate: readStoredJson catches read errors, and the inline rows still report. (3) Inline entries are judged as settings validation admits them: validation strips an inline value_schema (the field registers, so no row is due) and refuses a document with an unknown inline type (the settings read replaces it with defaults and reports it), so the scan reads validateSettings(...).data. (4) The registry normalizes a definition more than once, and stripFlagPrefix removed one dash pair per pass, so cli_flag '----' passed the single-pass inspection but was dropped by the registry; stripFlagPrefix now removes every leading pair in one pass, which matches the registry's final result and makes normalization idempotent. (5) Non-boolean repeatable, required, required_on_create and allow_unset values are reported with the behavior they leave in place. (6) Diagnostic formatting never throws (a BigInt or cyclic alias used to throw from JSON.stringify, which would have changed whether the field resolves). (7) The fix hint says add-field keeps a stored value_schema, so value_schema_invalid needs an edit of the file. Specs added in field-definition-losses.spec.ts (30 cases, 100% of the module)."
  "2026-11-27T04:40:02.557Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 (2026-11-27) on 9de3e2c90: 6 findings on this item. Fixed: (1) diagnostic formatting falls back to a constant when both JSON.stringify and String throw (an object with toString null and a BigInt member); (2) the scan takes the fields-file path from the stored settings, so a failed schema hydration that left the loaded schema at its defaults no longer scans the unused default file; (3) value_schema keywords the registry does not read (minLength instead of min_length, nested under properties, items and one_of) are reported with their path, since they are dropped without enforcement; (4) a non-boolean member is described by the value the registry reads (it reads as false or true), not by an overall behavior that another member can override; (5) the fix hint lists what add-field replaces and what it keeps (value_schema, metadata_key, front_matter_key, repeatable). Declined with reason: keeping the old one-pair-per-pass flag stripping. The old registry result for six or more leading dashes depended on how many normalization passes a path ran (inline and file-backed definitions run different counts), so it was never a stable contract; stripping every leading pair in one pass makes the inspection, the registry and every path agree, and is identical for every spelling with fewer than six leading dashes."
